that my costume consisted
of like some sort of vinyl.

00:24:32.180 --> 00:24:33.013
You know what I mean?

00:24:33.013 --> 00:24:33.846
Plastic.

00:24:33.846 --> 00:24:35.830
It was very plastic and if you recall.

00:24:35.830 --> 00:24:37.820
It was like a plastic tarp
with a hole that your head

00:24:37.820 --> 00:24:39.420
goes through and then a mask.

00:24:39.420 --> 00:24:42.900
If you recall your memory
of Big Bird you might not

00:24:42.900 --> 00:24:44.930
remember his legs.

00:24:44.930 --> 00:24:46.630
Remember the color of his legs?

00:24:46.630 --> 00:24:50.180
Orange legs with pink rings?

00:24:50.180 --> 00:24:52.310
Yeah, I would've just said it was orange

00:24:52.310 --> 00:24:53.660
and pink striped legs.

00:24:53.660 --> 00:24:55.560
Like horizontal stripes
that go up the leg,

00:24:55.560 --> 00:24:56.660
pink and orange, yeah.

00:24:57.620 --> 00:24:59.640
And that's what I remember.

00:24:59.640 --> 00:25:01.320
I'm not gonna bring up
a picture of Big Bird

00:25:01.320 --> 00:25:04.310
because I want this to be a
pristine thing in my mind.

00:25:04.310 --> 00:25:05.143
Okay.

00:25:06.050 --> 00:25:09.120
And literally the first
step out the door I tripped

00:25:09.120 --> 00:25:14.120
on something, fell down and
fell on my knee and ripped

00:25:16.500 --> 00:25:21.290
a hole in the vinyl and also
kinda skinned my knee up.

00:25:21.290 --> 00:25:24.400
And at that point it
was that I'm embarrassed

00:25:24.400 --> 00:25:28.440
because my hole, my
costume has a hole in it.

00:25:28.440 --> 00:25:30.660
Yeah I think you knew if
people looked at your knee

00:25:30.660 --> 00:25:32.476
they would know there's
a human under there.

00:25:32.476 --> 00:25:33.400
They're like that's not Big Bird I can see

00:25:33.400 --> 00:25:34.630
a human knee under there.

00:25:34.630 --> 00:25:35.463
Yeah.

00:25:35.463 --> 00:25:37.890
And so the whole plan had fallen apart

00:25:37.890 --> 00:25:42.720
and I just stayed in, what I
remember, again, I don't know

00:25:42.720 --> 00:25:46.400
what part is constructed and
what part actually happened.

00:25:46.400 --> 00:25:50.820
I remember staying inside
and they had to like bring,

00:25:50.820 --> 00:25:53.500
you know, like my brother would
bring me some of his candy

00:25:53.500 --> 00:25:55.180
like I had to use some of
his candy 'cause I didn't

00:25:55.180 --> 00:25:56.013
actually go outside.

00:25:56.013 --> 00:25:58.747
So you just stayed, you stayed
back with your dad probably

00:25:58.747 --> 00:26:01.660
and your mom took your brother out.

00:26:01.660 --> 00:26:02.730
Don't remember those details.

00:26:02.730 --> 00:26:04.630
I just told you everything
that I remember.

00:26:04.630 --> 00:26:06.210
Okay, so that's your first memory.

00:26:06.210 --> 00:26:07.830
There's a little trauma there.

00:26:07.830 --> 00:26:08.870
Maybe a lot of trauma.

00:26:08.870 --> 00:26:09.780
It seems like a lot.

00:26:09.780 --> 00:26:14.429
I'd be interested in what
your parents say about this.

00:26:14.429 --> 00:26:17.100
What I keep, when I was
walking across the parking lot

00:26:17.100 --> 00:26:20.040
this morning you were sitting
in your car on the phone.

00:26:20.040 --> 00:26:22.217
I thought I heard your mom's voice.

00:26:22.217 --> 00:26:23.050
You did.

00:26:23.050 --> 00:26:25.040
You were talking to your mom in the car?

00:26:26.050 --> 00:26:27.900
You should have asked her about this.

00:26:29.360 --> 00:26:32.000
Erm, I didn't, she's not gonna remember.

00:26:32.000 --> 00:26:33.830
You talking about something else?

00:26:33.830 --> 00:26:36.440
What else is there to talk about?

00:26:36.440 --> 00:26:39.300
My dad called me last
night, this is a side note,

00:26:39.300 --> 00:26:42.227
I mailed him finally, I mailed him

00:26:42.227 --> 00:26:46.780
"Lost Causes of Bleak Creek"
finally and I also gave him

00:26:46.780 --> 00:26:47.750
one of our records.

00:26:47.750 --> 00:26:50.660
You know Mythical Society
when we covered Merle

00:26:50.660 --> 00:26:54.210
and it comes, you know it
comes with that sticker in it

00:26:54.210 --> 00:26:56.300
that says Mythical Recording?

00:26:56.300 --> 00:26:58.160
And my dad was talking about
the record and he said he liked

00:26:58.160 --> 00:27:00.720
the album cover and all that
stuff and how if you turn

00:27:00.720 --> 00:27:02.063
it over it's the reverse.

00:27:03.923 --> 00:27:07.077
And then he said, "And now,
you know that little thing

00:27:07.077 --> 00:27:10.767
"that says 'Mythical Recording' on it?

00:27:10.767 --> 00:27:15.767
"If I were to peel that off
would that stick on something

00:27:20.837 --> 00:27:23.800
"or would that mess it up?"

00:27:23.800 --> 00:27:27.823
And I was like, "Yeah it's
fine dad it's a sticker.

00:27:29.147 --> 00:27:30.177
"It is a sticker.

00:27:30.177 --> 00:27:31.660
"I think that's the word
you're looking for."

00:27:31.660 --> 00:27:34.257
He was like, "Well okay,
I knew what a sticker was

00:27:34.257 --> 00:27:36.667
"I just didn't know if
this was a sticker."

00:27:38.741 --> 00:27:40.180
It sounds like you had
a much more interesting

00:27:40.180 --> 00:27:41.610
conversation than I did with my mom.

00:27:41.610 --> 00:27:43.800
We didn't talk about stickers at all.

00:27:43.800 --> 00:27:46.140
Well, so do you wanna hear my first memory

00:27:46.140 --> 00:27:48.132
and then we'll analyze both of 'em?

00:27:48.132 --> 00:27:48.965
Okay.

00:27:48.965 --> 00:27:51.780
My first memory was, let's see,

00:27:51.780 --> 00:27:53.620
before I went to preschool.

00:27:53.620 --> 00:27:54.857
So I went to preschool when I was like six

00:27:54.857 --> 00:27:56.590
and then, or five?

00:27:56.590 --> 00:27:58.630
No I went to kindergarten
when I was six, preschool

00:27:58.630 --> 00:28:02.740
when I was like five, so from
the age of like two to four

00:28:02.740 --> 00:28:05.263
I stayed at Retta's house.

00:28:06.410 --> 00:28:10.740
Loretta, you met her, she
was my first babysitter.

00:28:10.740 --> 00:28:14.030
She only kept me and my
first best friend Brad

00:28:14.030 --> 00:28:16.760
that I talk about in
the book of Mythicality

00:28:16.760 --> 00:28:21.760
and so my first memories are
at her house where I would

00:28:22.580 --> 00:28:23.653
spend the day.

00:28:25.360 --> 00:28:27.280
I remember one day we would
always stay at her house

00:28:27.280 --> 00:28:31.550
we would rarely leave and maybe
that's why I remember this

00:28:31.550 --> 00:28:34.110
because it was very unusual
because we did leave.

00:28:34.110 --> 00:28:39.010
We went to a friend of hers
house and it was maybe a mile

00:28:39.010 --> 00:28:42.930
or two away still a way in the
country and there was this,

00:28:42.930 --> 00:28:45.140
we were playing in the
backyard and there were some

00:28:45.140 --> 00:28:46.650
other kids there.

00:28:46.650 --> 00:28:50.780
I remember being very
afraid of other kids.

00:28:50.780 --> 00:28:53.063
Like even in preschool.

00:28:54.250 --> 00:28:57.790
I don't know, I liked the
people that I knew man and--

00:28:57.790 --> 00:28:59.255
But what were you afraid of?

00:28:59.255 --> 00:29:00.088
I don't know.

00:29:00.088 --> 00:29:00.921
What was gonna happen?

00:29:00.921 --> 00:29:01.834
I don't know.

00:29:01.834 --> 00:29:04.084
Just the peopleness of 'em.

00:29:05.773 --> 00:29:08.063
I don't know, I haven't gotten into that.

00:29:09.360 --> 00:29:13.500
But I do remember I was in
the backyard and I was on the

00:29:13.500 --> 00:29:15.730
swing set thing and you know
they got like normal swings

00:29:15.730 --> 00:29:19.260
and they're like metal swings
but you've got this certain

00:29:19.260 --> 00:29:23.560
type of swing that it's got two
benches that face each other

00:29:23.560 --> 00:29:28.200
and then so as it swings
it kinda stays parallel.

00:29:28.200 --> 00:29:29.033
You know what I'm talking about?

00:29:29.033 --> 00:29:32.300
Like a very old-timey type swing.

00:29:32.300 --> 00:29:35.400
But it's two people facing
each other on this thing.

00:29:35.400 --> 00:29:37.187
You never been on one of those?

00:29:38.080 --> 00:29:39.510
And you stay--
You were probably too tall.

00:29:39.510 --> 00:29:42.090
You stay at the same height?

00:29:42.090 --> 00:29:46.230
You stay parallel to the
ground but you move up and down

00:29:46.230 --> 00:29:50.730
'cause it's kinda like
a rowboat kinda vibe.

00:29:50.730 --> 00:29:52.870
I don know it's kinda, there's two.

00:29:52.870 --> 00:29:54.400
I think you're making this up.

00:29:54.400 --> 00:29:55.714
There's two.
I think even the swing

00:29:55.714 --> 00:29:56.810
is a fabrication.
There's two bars.

00:29:56.810 --> 00:29:58.900
There's two bars that come down.

00:29:58.900 --> 00:30:02.900
One in front of one person and
one beside the other person.

00:30:02.900 --> 00:30:04.180
Oh, it's not on a chain?

00:30:04.180 --> 00:30:07.654
No it's on bars and they're
fixed and you're facing

00:30:07.654 --> 00:30:09.063
the other person.

00:30:09.063 --> 00:30:09.896
Okay.

00:30:09.896 --> 00:30:12.980
I was on it by myself and
I was holding onto the bars

00:30:12.980 --> 00:30:13.813
and I remember--

00:30:13.813 --> 00:30:14.646
Facing no one.

00:30:14.646 --> 00:30:18.670
It like, I'm pretty sure it
pinched or the way I would

00:30:18.670 --> 00:30:21.870
have said it at the
time was, it pinched me.

00:30:21.870 --> 00:30:26.840
It pinched my hand and
cut it and there was blood

00:30:26.840 --> 00:30:29.800
and my recollection is
there was a lot of blood

00:30:29.800 --> 00:30:32.250
and I remember running
into this stranger's house

00:30:32.250 --> 00:30:35.450
and trying to find Retta and--

00:30:35.450 --> 00:30:37.280
Well first of all before
you continue I will say

00:30:37.280 --> 00:30:42.180
that they don't make jungle gym
equipment like they used to.

00:30:42.180 --> 00:30:43.013
Oh no.

00:30:43.013 --> 00:30:45.670
I actually believe,
everything is so safe now,

00:30:45.670 --> 00:30:49.860
I think that there should be
a way that a kid could come

00:30:49.860 --> 00:30:51.900
away from a piece of equipment bleeding

00:30:51.900 --> 00:30:52.860
just as a life lesson.

00:30:52.860 --> 00:30:54.690
Maybe with one less ear.

00:30:54.690 --> 00:30:58.760
Yeah, I mean I think that
every piece of playground

00:30:58.760 --> 00:31:01.130
equipment that is manufactured
now should have like

00:31:01.130 --> 00:31:04.660
one really unnecessarily sharp point.

00:31:04.660 --> 00:31:06.000
Yeah, like a jagged edge.

00:31:06.000 --> 00:31:09.070
So that at least once a year
one kid really gets snagged

00:31:09.070 --> 00:31:09.903
on that thing.

00:31:09.903 --> 00:31:12.160
And then the custodian should
come out with a paint brush

00:31:12.160 --> 00:31:14.700
of tetanus and just
kinda like brush it on.

00:31:14.700 --> 00:31:17.320
Well tetanus is probably over
kill but I think you gotta

00:31:17.320 --> 00:31:19.830
have some blood and some scars.

00:31:19.830 --> 00:31:21.100
What else are you gonna remember?

00:31:21.100 --> 00:31:22.610
As we're demonstrating here.

00:31:22.610 --> 00:31:27.173
So I run into Retta and I love this woman.

00:31:28.811 --> 00:31:30.623
She's very short.

00:31:31.580 --> 00:31:35.900
I think I bond with people
who are of extreme heights.

00:31:35.900 --> 00:31:37.860
My wife's pretty tall for her height.

00:31:37.860 --> 00:31:38.693
For her height?

00:31:40.820 --> 00:31:42.230
For her age, I don't know
what I was gonna say.

00:31:42.230 --> 00:31:44.460
Your pretty tall for your height baby.

00:31:44.460 --> 00:31:45.683
For her sex.

00:31:47.060 --> 00:31:50.840
Okay, you could say
pretty tall for a woman.

00:31:50.840 --> 00:31:53.270
Okay, yeah, yeah, she's a woman.

00:31:53.270 --> 00:31:54.583
That's beside the point.

00:31:55.760 --> 00:31:57.030
Loretta was very short.

00:31:57.030 --> 00:31:59.536
Sometimes the way out--

00:31:59.536 --> 00:32:00.369
She's like four, 10.

00:32:00.369 --> 00:32:02.123
Is a lot easier than you make it.

00:32:03.331 --> 00:32:05.610
Loretta was like four, 10.

00:32:05.610 --> 00:32:10.030
She was like the most
approachable person to a kid

00:32:10.030 --> 00:32:11.453
and she made me feel better.

00:32:12.640 --> 00:32:13.767
That's my first memory.

00:32:13.767 --> 00:32:15.108
Did she kiss it?

00:32:15.108 --> 00:32:15.941
I don't think.

00:32:15.941 --> 00:32:16.774
Did she kiss your boo-boo?

00:32:16.774 --> 00:32:18.500
She cleaned it up, she
cleaned up the boo-boo.

00:32:18.500 --> 00:32:22.440
And I don't remember Brad
being there but I remember,

00:32:22.440 --> 00:32:26.110
my second memory, and again
it may have come first

00:32:26.110 --> 00:32:29.910
I really have no way to know,
was the one I tell in the book

00:32:29.910 --> 00:32:32.610
of Mythicality and that's I
go into the bathroom right

00:32:32.610 --> 00:32:35.990
after he came out of the
bathroom and he forgot to flush

00:32:35.990 --> 00:32:40.620
and there was a turd in
there that was bright orange

00:32:40.620 --> 00:32:44.403
and I will never forget
that Cheeto colored turd.

00:32:45.540 --> 00:32:46.860
It shocked me.

00:32:46.860 --> 00:32:49.122
You gotta have something
sensational like an orange turd

00:32:49.122 --> 00:32:51.210
is definitely something that's
not gonna leave your brain.

00:32:51.210 --> 00:32:55.900
It was like a guy directing
traffic around a construction

00:32:55.900 --> 00:32:58.400
site on the street, you know.

00:32:58.400 --> 00:33:01.280
You gotta wear the most
like fluorescent orange

00:33:02.230 --> 00:33:03.720
vest type situation.

00:33:03.720 --> 00:33:04.856
What if it--

00:33:04.856 --> 00:33:06.120
It was like he crapped
a construction vest.

00:33:06.120 --> 00:33:06.953
But what if it wasn't a turd?

00:33:06.953 --> 00:33:08.080
Have you ever thought about that?

00:33:08.080 --> 00:33:09.210
It was a turd man.

00:33:09.210 --> 00:33:12.350
Are you sure that it wasn't
just like a giant Cheeto

00:33:12.350 --> 00:33:17.231
or like a cheese puff of some kind?

00:33:17.231 --> 00:33:19.045
That he was like I don't
wanna eat this and he threw it

00:33:19.045 --> 00:33:20.844
in the toilet and it
expanded to turd size.

00:33:20.844 --> 00:33:22.557
You talking about a Cheeto
the size of a baby's arm.

00:33:23.419 --> 00:33:25.419
That's how big it was?

00:33:26.980 --> 00:33:29.320
Yeah man, I'll never forget it.

00:33:29.320 --> 00:33:33.820
So one of those two are my first memory

00:33:33.820 --> 00:33:37.730
and they're equally traumatic
and I think that's the common

00:33:40.220 --> 00:33:42.360
denominator here between our two stories.

00:33:42.360 --> 00:33:45.690
Right, and I don't have any
other memories from Georgia

00:33:45.690 --> 00:33:48.010
which is where I spent
my first three years.

00:33:48.010 --> 00:33:52.490
It's funny because I would
have told you that I remembered

00:33:52.490 --> 00:33:56.610
when it snowed that winter
when I was three years old

00:33:57.460 --> 00:34:01.070
but I don't actually remember
it I've just seen the pictures

00:34:01.070 --> 00:34:02.720
because I do.

00:34:02.720 --> 00:34:06.360
I've seen the pictures of my
mom, I've seen the pictures

00:34:06.360 --> 00:34:11.360
of me and my brother with
plastic bags tied over our feet.

00:34:11.370 --> 00:34:12.853
It's funny like we were in south Georgia--

00:34:12.853 --> 00:34:14.670
You were very ill prepared.

00:34:14.670 --> 00:34:17.790
We're in south Georgia and my mom's idea

00:34:17.790 --> 00:34:22.790
of like keeping us safe in
the snow is to put slippery

00:34:23.570 --> 00:34:26.710
plastic bags around our feet
and send us out into the snow.

00:34:26.710 --> 00:34:28.490
You don't want your feet to get wet.

00:34:28.490 --> 00:34:31.020
Right, you just want to
fall and break something.

00:34:31.020 --> 00:34:33.630
I think when you're a parent
especially, I'm not gonna,

00:34:33.630 --> 00:34:36.530
I'm not gonna project this
on your mom and assume

00:34:36.530 --> 00:34:39.310
that she wasn't actually thinking
this, but especially back

00:34:39.310 --> 00:34:41.620
then, you know, you'd put
your kids out the front door

00:34:41.620 --> 00:34:43.950
and you'd say, "Don't come
back for a couple of hours."

00:34:43.950 --> 00:34:45.960
Matter of fact there's a couple
of times that I've even said

00:34:45.960 --> 00:34:47.120
that to my kids now.

00:34:47.120 --> 00:34:47.953
I'm like, you know what?

00:34:47.953 --> 00:34:49.823
I'm fed up with these screens.

00:34:50.870 --> 00:34:55.870
Go outside and do not come back
in for and I'll say an hour.

00:34:55.950 --> 00:34:57.050
An hour's a long time.

00:34:57.050 --> 00:35:00.130
But like 15 minutes later
they ring the doorbell.

00:35:00.130 --> 00:35:04.700
Well if you send 'em out in
the snow and you don't put

00:35:04.700 --> 00:35:09.330
bags over their socks and
shoes then once those socks

00:35:09.330 --> 00:35:13.177
get wet, "I gotta come inside mom.

00:35:13.177 --> 00:35:16.210
"It's Rhett, it's your Big Bird boy."

00:35:16.210 --> 00:35:17.043
First of all I was three

00:35:17.043 --> 00:35:18.955
I'm pretty sure she didn't
I gotta go back inside.

00:35:18.955 --> 00:35:20.860
leave me and it was snowing
in Georgia which was very

00:35:20.860 --> 00:35:23.250
unusual and I think that's why
I thought that I remember it

00:35:23.250 --> 00:35:25.030
but I think it's just a picture.

00:35:25.030 --> 00:35:28.120
You think your mom put plastic
bags around her own shoes?

00:35:28.120 --> 00:35:28.953
No.

00:35:28.953 --> 00:35:30.840
It's like Reeboks that are back

00:35:30.840 --> 00:35:32.511
in style now.
I don't think so.

00:35:32.511 --> 00:35:36.140
The additional things that I
remember is that there's just

00:35:37.000 --> 00:35:42.000
sporadic things from my time
in Thousand Oaks, California.

00:35:42.280 --> 00:35:43.460
What's your first memory from there?

00:35:43.460 --> 00:35:45.210
So this will be your second memory.

00:35:46.420 --> 00:35:50.613
I honestly can not put these
in chronological order.

00:35:51.970 --> 00:35:53.320
I don't know.

00:35:53.320 --> 00:35:56.250
Because I remember being
taught how to ride a bike

00:35:56.250 --> 00:36:01.250
and falling and falling a few
houses down and then my pants

00:36:07.090 --> 00:36:11.880
getting caught in the chain
and not being able to move

00:36:11.880 --> 00:36:14.267
and having to yell for
my dad to come after me.

00:36:14.267 --> 00:36:18.640
And that's probably, I
probably combined two memories

00:36:18.640 --> 00:36:20.710
because there was when I
was learning to ride my bike

00:36:20.710 --> 00:36:23.440
and then me riding my
bike probably by myself

00:36:23.440 --> 00:36:24.390
and that happening.

00:36:26.160 --> 00:36:29.100
I remember a giant sunflower.

00:36:29.100 --> 00:36:29.960
What?

00:36:29.960 --> 00:36:33.920
Yeah this is the only
good memory that I have

00:36:33.920 --> 00:36:35.810
from back, from really early.

00:36:35.810 --> 00:36:37.880
You know what if you really
look at a giant sunflower

00:36:37.880 --> 00:36:39.990
from up close I would
argue that it's actually

00:36:39.990 --> 00:36:41.690
pretty damn scary.

00:36:41.690 --> 00:36:42.910
They're kinda wicked.

00:36:42.910 --> 00:36:44.830
When was the last time you
stared down a sunflower?

00:36:44.830 --> 00:36:48.010
It's like the black part
